   "source": [
    "Interpretation:\n",
    "The image contains four hexbin density plots that illustrate the relationships between Global Horizontal Irradiance (GHI), Ambient Temperature, Relative Humidity, and Wind Speed. These charts are commonly used in solar energy and meteorological analysis to visualize the distribution and density of data points across different variables.\n",
    "1. GHI vs. Ambient Temperature\n",
    "This plot shows the relationship between solar radiation (GHI) and the surrounding temperature.\n",
    "Density: The highest density of data points (red/orange) is concentrated at zero GHI with temperatures between 20°C and 30°C, likely representing nighttime or very cloudy conditions.\n",
    "Trend: As GHI increases (moving right on the x-axis), the range of ambient temperatures shifts upward, indicating that higher solar radiation is generally associated with warmer temperatures, though there is significant spread.\n",
    "2. GHI vs. Relative Humidity\n",
    "This chart examines how solar intensity relates to the moisture in the air.\n",
    "Density: There is a very high concentration of data points at zero GHI across a wide range of humidity levels (from 10% to 100%).\n",
    "Trend: Higher GHI values (above 800) are predominantly associated with lower relative humidity (below 40%). This suggests that the clearest, sunniest days often occur when the air is relatively dry.\n",
    "3. Temperature vs. Humidity\n",
    "This plot visualizes the classic inverse relationship between temperature and moisture.\n",
    "Density: The most frequent occurrences (yellow/bright orange) are found at temperatures between 22°C and 25°C with high humidity (above 90%).\n",
    "Trend: There is a clear diagonal trend showing that as Ambient Temperature increases, Relative Humidity tends to decrease. This is a standard meteorological pattern where warmer air can hold more water vapor, leading to lower relative humidity if the absolute moisture remains constant.\n",
    "4. Wind Speed vs. GHI\n",
    "This final plot compares wind conditions with solar radiation.\n",
    "Density: The highest density is found at very low wind speeds (near 0) and zero GHI.\n",
    "Trend: The data is broadly distributed, but it shows that GHI can reach its maximum (around 1200) across a variety of wind speeds (0 to 6+). There doesn't appear to be a strong linear correlation between these two variables, though the highest GHI values seem slightly more frequent at moderate wind speeds.\n",
    "Variable\n",
    "Range Observed\n",
    "Key Observation\n",
    "GHI\n",
    "0 to ~1200 $W/m^2$\n",
    "Most data points are at 0 (nighttime).\n",
    "Ambient Temperature\n",
    "~15°C to ~42°C\n",
    "Positively correlated with GHI.\n",
    "Relative Humidity\n",
    "0% to 100%\n",
    "Negatively correlated with Temperature and GHI.\n",
    "Wind Speed\n",
    "0 to ~7 m/s\n",
    "Mostly concentrated at low speeds.\n"
   ]
