    "## Interpretation of the Correlation Matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "The correlation matrix and heatmap provide insights into the linear relationships between the main solar and weather variables:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Strong Positive Correlations:**\n",
    "  - GHI, DNI, and DHI (solar radiation variables) are highly correlated with each other, as expected.\n",
    "  - Module temperatures (TModA, TModB) and ambient temperature (Tamb) are also strongly correlated, indicating that module temperature closely follows ambient conditions.\n",
    "- **Negative Correlations:**\n",
    "  - Relative Humidity (RH) shows a negative correlation with solar radiation variables (GHI, DNI, DHI), meaning that higher humidity is generally associated with lower solar irradiance (cloudier conditions).\n",
    "  - Wind Speed (WS) and module/ambient temperatures have weak to moderate negative correlations, suggesting that higher wind speeds may help cool the modules and ambient air.\n",
    "- **Weak or No Correlation:**\n",
    "  - Barometric Pressure (BP) does not show strong correlation with most other variables, indicating it is relatively independent in this context.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Key Takeaway:**\n",
    "- The matrix helps identify which variables are most closely related, which is useful for feature selection, multicollinearity checks, and understanding the physical relationships in the solar dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## Simplified Multivariate Analysis Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The pairplot and scatter plots show how key variables interact with each other:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **GHI vs Temperature:** Positive relationship—higher solar radiation is observed on hotter days.\n",
    "- **GHI vs Humidity:** Negative relationship—higher humidity (cloudier conditions) is linked to lower solar radiation.\n",
    "- **Temperature vs Humidity:** Generally, as temperature increases, humidity decreases, which is typical in clear, sunny conditions.\n",
    "- **Wind Speed vs GHI:** No strong relationship, but wind can slightly reduce module/ambient temperatures.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How to Read the Pairplot:**\n",
    "- Each diagonal plot shows the distribution of a single variable.\n",
    "- Each off-diagonal plot shows the relationship between two variables.\n",
    "- Look for clear trends (upward, downward, or clusters) to spot strong relationships.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Summary:**\n",
    "- Most relationships are as expected for solar data: solar radiation increases with temperature and decreases with humidity.\n",
    "- No strong outliers or unexpected patterns are visible, indicating the data is consistent and clean for further analysis."
